WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:16.470 align:start position:0%
 
good morning  and welcome to all those residents who  are watching via the council's live  stream  on friday the 9th of april 2021  we received the very sad news that his  royal highness of prince philip duke of  edinburgh  had passed away at the age of 99.  the funeral service is scheduled to take  place tomorrow saturday the 16th of  april at 3 pm  and will be preceded by our national  minute silence  members of rocha district council have  therefore gathered this morning in honor  of his royal highness the prince philip  duke of edinburgh  and i will shortly be inviting group  leaders or their representatives to say  a few words before we take part in rocha  district council's two minutes silence  prince philip's contribution to our  nation serving loyally  at the side of her majesty the queen for  more than 70 years  cannot be underestimated in addition  the young people in our district have  benefited significantly from  participating in the duke of edinburgh's  award scheme for a number of years  a scheme founded by him which has  enabled many young people to build the  skills  confidence and resilience they have  needed in order to make the most  of their lives our thoughts are with her  majesty the queen  i have written to the queen's private  secretary on behalf of rochford district  council  to express condolences to the queen  and all the royal family  we have seized apologies for today from  councillor mr hoy councillor mrs  christine mason  councillor newport and council wilkinson  councillor wootten can i please ask you  to say a few words  thank you chairman and i would like to  echo and very much support  the sentiments you have expressed  especially in respect of the devotion to  duty  and support to her majesty the queen  which his royal highness duke of  edinburgh gave over  many many years  after so many very eloquent tribute  speeches  which we have heard over the past few  days  as leader of rochford district council  there is absolutely nothing  i can sensibly add today to adequately  sum up honoring  the extraordinary life of the duke of  edinburgh  in a life where he achieved so much  whilst it is a privilege to be a  community representative  we as counselors know only too well  being in the public eye does not come  without challenge  and comment at times especially if  you're a bit of a character  with a sense of humor as undoubtedly the  duke of edinburgh was  his commitment and devotion to public  service  was simply amazing and beyond question  albeit that like us some of his comments  got him into trouble from time to time  the tribute which has most struck a  chord with me  is from his daughter princess anne  she said my father has been my teacher  my supporter and my critic but mostly  it is his life well-lived and service  freely given  that i most wanted to emulate  his ability to treat every person  as an individual in their own right with  their own  skills comes through all the  organizations  with which he was involved  in conclusion i believe we as  individuals could well heed  some of those values in the way in which  we lead our lives  and that we encourage people to  contribute to the charities  to enable the good work he started to  continue  that would be the finest practical  legacy and tribute  we could pay to his royal highness the  duke of edinburgh  sir you have served this country proudly  rest in peace thank you chairman  thank you council button i shall now  i shall now ask councillor adrian eaves  deputy leader of the rocha district  residence  to say a few words counselor eaves  thank you chairman there's been much  written and commented  on his royal highness prince philip duke  of edinburgh  since his passing a man known for his  straight talking and military past  a husband of 73 years which in itself is  to be applauded in this day and age  i want to concentrate on one  extraordinary element of  prince philip's life my mother who  passed away only a couple of months ago  was a staunch royalist  she was known to go to london for any  royal related events  trooping the colour the coronation  various commemorations and the like  she took great pride in telling us his  children how she slept on the street in  london on the occasion of the royal  wedding all those many years ago  just to make sure she had a good good  spot to view the proceedings  at all these events prince philip was by  the queen's side  he never upstaged the queen but it was  always there to support her  prince philip had a difficult childhood  and one that most would have found  challenging  particularly difficult must have been  over his mother with her struggles with  mental illness  in the prince's early years  it is often said these days that behind  every successful man is a strong woman  in the case of the queen and prince  philip it could be said that this was  the case but the other way around  in many ways that made prince philip an  early trailblazer  for men to be supporting women in  powerful positions these days  not unusual these days but much less so  when the queen and prince philip were  married  no one can deny the unfailing support  that he had given the queen  in all their many years together through  the good and bad  indeed the queen acknowledged it in her  announcement soon after he passed away  perhaps then we can learn from prince  philip's example in our own lives  putting others first is not a weakness  but an attribute to aspire to which  results in a higher attainment for all  those around us  for being that role model i suppose  salute the man  we all knew as his royal highness prince  philip  duke of edinburgh may he rest in peace  thank you councillor eves um counselor  newport leader of the liberal  democrat party is unable to be with us  today his words  will be spoken by angela hutchings  acting man  director of rochford district council  angela thank you chairman his royal  highness prince philip  was the embodiment of service in our  country for over 80 years  he was one of the last wartime heroes  and throughout the post-war period  he championed robust individualism a  trait that occasionally got him into  trouble  he had many important achievements one  very significant accomplishment which  bore his name and continued undiminished  to this day  is the duke of edinburgh awards scheme  for young people  one of the local liberal democrat family  daniel erlund was a recipient of the  duke of edinburgh award in his youth  his challenges were as varied as looking  after an old gentleman  sports training and a long trek over  several days  camping cooking and orienteering  the duke perceptively saw that these  sorts of challenges would build  confidence and self-esteem in young  people  stretching them in many ways and  equipping them for life and the many  challenges it would bring  what better memorial can a man have than  to provide a path to good citizenship  thank you chairman thank you angela  hutchings  there will now be two minutes silence  foreign thank you all members  officers and residents for attending  today  thank you

